<h1>Advanced Topics</h1>

<p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
  Dynamic overrides, blueprint extension, injection context, semi-dynamic services,
    and service retrieval.
</p>

<h2>Dynamic overrides</h2>

<p>When creating a container from a blueprint, you can override dynamic services with specific instances. This is essential for request-scoped data in web applications:</p>

<Code code={`class RequestContext(val userId: String)

class UserService(val ctx: RequestContext) {
    fun currentUser() = "User: ${'$'}{ctx.userId}"
}

val blueprint = kontainer {
    dynamic(RequestContext::class)
    singleton(UserService::class)
}

// Override the dynamic service when creating a container
val kontainer = blueprint.create {
    with(RequestContext::class) { RequestContext("user-42") }
}

println(kontainer.get(UserService::class).currentUser())
// "User: user-42"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<ul>
  <li>Only <code>dynamic</code> services can be overridden. An override of anything else, a <code>dynamicPrototype</code> included, makes <code>create</code> throw <code>KontainerInconsistent</code> before any override lambda runs.</li>
  <li>The lambda runs at <code>create</code>, once per container. A caller who needs laziness builds it into the value it hands in, as <code>UserProvider.lazy {'{'} {'}'}</code> of the <code>ultra:security</code> module does.</li>
  <li><code>with(X::class)</code> replaces <code>X</code> when <code>X</code> is itself a registered dynamic service, otherwise the one dynamic service <code>X</code> is a subtype of; with several it throws <code>ServiceAmbiguous</code>. Getting or injecting a type that several dynamic services implement stays ambiguous.</li>
  <li>Two overrides for the same service under different classes, <code>with(Base::class)</code> and <code>with(Derived::class)</code>, both run, and the later one wins.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Semi-dynamic services</h2>

<p>If a singleton injects a dynamic service, Kontainer automatically promotes it to <strong>semi-dynamic</strong> — it becomes a singleton within each container instance rather than global. This happens transparently; you don't need to do anything.</p>

<Code code={`class RequestContext(val userId: String)

// UserService is defined as singleton, but it injects a dynamic service.
// Kontainer promotes it to semi-dynamic automatically.
class UserService(val ctx: RequestContext)

val blueprint = kontainer {
    dynamic(RequestContext::class)
    singleton(UserService::class)  // automatically becomes semi-dynamic
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>Blueprint extension</h2>

<p>Create a new blueprint by extending an existing one. The original is not modified:</p>

<Code code={`val base = kontainer {
    singleton(Counter::class)
}

val extended = base.extend {
    singleton(MyService::class)
}

// 'extended' has both Counter and MyService
// 'base' still only has Counter`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p>A service defined again in the extension follows the same <a href="/ultra/kontainer/modules">redefinition rule</a> as within one blueprint.</p>

<h2>Cloning containers</h2>

<p>Clone a container to get a fresh instance with reset dynamic services. The overrides given at <code>create</code> are kept and their lambdas run again; new overrides take precedence. <code>clone</code> runs the same checks as <code>create</code>:</p>

<Code code={`val kontainer = blueprint.create {
    with(RequestContext::class) { RequestContext("user-1") }
}

// Clone with different dynamic overrides
val cloned = kontainer.clone {
    with(RequestContext::class) { RequestContext("user-2") }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>Injecting the container and blueprint</h2>

<p>The <code>Kontainer</code> itself and the <code>KontainerBlueprint</code> are automatically available as services. Any service can inject them:</p>

<Code code={`// Injects the Kontainer, which is dynamic: one ServiceLocator per container
class ServiceLocator(val kontainer: Kontainer) {
    fun <T : Any> resolve(cls: KClass<T>): T = kontainer.get(cls)
}

class BlueprintAware(val blueprint: KontainerBlueprint) {
    fun createChild() = blueprint.create()
}

val blueprint = kontainer {
    singleton(ServiceLocator::class)
    singleton(BlueprintAware::class)
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p><code>Kontainer</code>, <code>KontainerBlueprint</code> and <code>InjectionContext</code> are managed by the container itself. Registering one of them, in any form, or overriding one at <code>create</code> throws <code>KontainerInconsistent</code>.</p>

<h2>Injection context</h2>

<p>Services can inject <code>InjectionContext</code> to know who is requesting them and what is being injected. Useful for logging and context-aware behavior:</p>

<Code code={`class ContextAwareService(val ctx: InjectionContext) {
    fun whoAsked() = ctx.requestingClass.simpleName
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>Retrieving services</h2>

<p>Kontainer provides several ways to get services:</p>

<Code code={`val kontainer = blueprint.create()

// Get — throws if not found
val service = kontainer.get(MyService::class)

// Get with reified type
val service2 = kontainer.get<MyService>()

// GetOrNull — returns null if not found
val maybe = kontainer.getOrNull(MyService::class)

// Use — runs block only if service exists
kontainer.use(MyService::class) {
    println(this.doSomething())
}

// GetAll — all implementations of a type, each as itself
val allRepos: List<Repository> = kontainer.getAll(Repository::class)

// GetLookup — lazy lookup of implementations
val lookup: LazyServiceLookup<Repository> = kontainer.getLookup(Repository::class)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

